time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f(x) = y$, how can $x$ be written using the inverse function?","options":["$x = f(y)$","$x = y^{-1}$","$x = 1/f'(y)$","$x = f^{-1}(y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"374478700700b93bebba0111","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f'(x_0) = 2$ and $y_0 = f(x_0)$, what is $(f^{-1})'(y_0)$?","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$2$","$0$","$-2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a860ae76858fd83123898ae4","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f'(x_0) = 5$ and $y_0 = f(x_0)$, what does the formula give for $(f^{-1})'(y_0)$?","options":["$5$","$-5$","$\\frac{1}{5}$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a57cba4ed0fe52821e0c6234","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition on $f'(x)$ is required in the Inverse Function Theorem?","options":["$f'(x) \\neq 0$","$f'(x) = 0$","$f'(x) = 1$ always.","$f'(x)$ must be undefined."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"47a7d9033547015c62362b3b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which additional assumption about $f^{-1}$ appears in the Inverse Function Theorem?","options":["$f^{-1}$ is continuous at $y$.","$f^{-1}$ is constant at $y$.","$f^{-1}$ is not defined at $y$.","$f^{-1}$ has derivative $0$ at $y$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5531974e38891ec5f3fadc0e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_007","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be a differentiable bijection and $y_0=f(x_0)$. Which conclusion is guaranteed?","options":["$f^{-1}$ is differentiable at every point of $\\mathbb{R}$.","If $f'(x_0)>1$, then $(f^{-1})'(y_0)>1$.","If $f'(x_0)=0$, then $(f^{-1})'(y_0)=0$.","If $f'(x_0)\\neq0$, then $(f^{-1})'(y_0)=\\frac{1}{f'(x_0)}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"41f16244583101a91f45ad1c","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x_0) = y_0$ and $f'(x_0) = -3$, which value does the Inverse Function Theorem give for $(f^{-1})'(y_0)$?","options":["$3$","$-\\frac{1}{3}$","$-3$","$\\frac{1}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"83b6b1e46e6d01f0c7032de0","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"At what kind of point can the inverse function theorem fail to apply?","options":["The theorem requires $f'(x) = 0$.","The theorem does not require $f$ to be invertible.","The theorem proves $f^{-1}$ is continuous without assumptions.","The theorem cannot be applied at a point where $f'(x) = 0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"92b1f187ab32f0f6b1edbed0","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement best connects the Inverse Function Theorem with the prerequisite concept of inverse functions?","options":["The derivative of $f^{-1}$ is evaluated at $x$, where $x = f(y)$.","The derivative of $f^{-1}$ is evaluated at $y$, where $y = f(x)$.","The inverse function is unnecessary in the formula.","The theorem applies even when no inverse exists."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b19b13993f3a344986171944","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ satisfy the Inverse Function Theorem assumptions at $x = 2$, with $f(2) = 7$ and $f'(2) = \\frac{1}{3}$. What is $(f^{-1})'(7)$?","options":["$3$","$\\frac{1}{3}$","$7$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4d12b66d616281799016793e","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $f'(x_0)=0$, which inverse-function-theorem conclusion is justified at $y_0=f(x_0)$?","options":["A zero derivative makes the inverse's derivative zero as well.","The theorem gives no derivative formula at this point.","The formula gives $(f^{-1})'(y_0)=1/0=\\infty$.","It shows that $f$ has no inverse near $y_0=f(x_0)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8a551038bd3dde21cc321b03","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the formula for $(f^{-1})'(y)$ use from the original function?","options":["The reciprocal of $f$ itself at the corresponding input","The reciprocal of $f'$ evaluated at $y$ itself","The negative of $f'$ at the corresponding input","The reciprocal of $f'$ at the corresponding input"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"48a020ed4121945a2fada257","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which point of $f$ corresponds to the point $y$ of $f^{-1}$?","options":["$x=f(y)$","$x=1/f(y)$","$x=(f^{-1})'(y)$","$x=f^{-1}(y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"50000b9e4c403ade60fd5bee","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must $f'(x)\\neq0$ appear in the Inverse Function Theorem?","options":["It guarantees that $f$ is invertible on $X$","It makes $f^{-1}$ continuous at $y$ automatically","The reciprocal $1/f'(x)$ must be defined","It ensures that $f$ is increasing near $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"824e3f8e0bcc74e7dc1143b9","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f'(x_0)=10$ and $y_0=f(x_0)$, what is $(f^{-1})'(y_0)$?","options":["$1/10$","$10$","$-10$","$-1/10$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c7d1d2d0243cd7a9c5f6b131","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f'(x_0)=-4$ and $y_0=f(x_0)$, what is $(f^{-1})'(y_0)$?","options":["$1/4$","$4$","$-1/4$","$-4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5206cb13b7aecb45a1c2e05d","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f^{-1}(9)=2$ and $f'(2)=6$, what is $(f^{-1})'(9)$?","options":["$6$","$2/9$","$1/6$","$9/2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"04dc5c22ac7cbb3c0d7cf697","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which assumptions are needed before concluding differentiability of $f^{-1}$ at $y$?","options":["Invertibility, differentiability of $f$ at $x$, $f'(x)\\neq0$, and continuity of $f$ at $x$","Invertibility, differentiability of $f$ at $x$, $f'(x)=0$, and continuity of $f^{-1}$ at $y$","Invertibility, differentiability of $f$ at $x$, $f'(x)\\neq0$, and continuity of $f^{-1}$ at $y$","Differentiability of $f$ on all of $X$ and $f'(x)\\neq0$, with no condition on $f^{-1}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"062affd4afb5bdb1e1ce6806","time_limit":50},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_inverse_function_theorem_2c26d705_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $f^{-1}(y)=3$ and $f'(3)=1/5$. What is $(f^{-1})'(y)$?","options":["$5$","$1/5$","$3/5$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cfec9ee16990aa75718b7d06","time_limit":30}]}}